Use a sliding window with two indices and a `Set`. First convert the input to the units whose uniqueness you mean; `Array.from(text)` iterates Unicode code points, while direct string indexing uses UTF-16 code units. Extend the right edge one unit at a time. If it is already present, remove units from the left until it is unique, then update the best length. Each unit is inserted and removed at most once, so the time complexity is O(n), not O(n²), and extra space is O(min(n, distinct-unit-count)). Clarify case sensitivity and whether grapheme clusters, rather than code points, are required.

```ts caption="A linear sliding-window solution that keeps indexing consistent with Unicode code-point iteration."
function longestUnique(text: string): number {
  const chars = Array.from(text);
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  let left = 0;
  let best = 0;

  for (const char of chars) {
    while (seen.has(char)) {
      seen.delete(chars[left++]);
    }
    seen.add(char);
    best = Math.max(best, seen.size);
  }
  return best;
}
```

Use a `Set` of stable record IDs to test membership and an output array for order. For each record, add it only if its ID is not already present. This is O(n) average-time for n records and O(n) additional space. Use `Map` when the ID should directly retrieve or replace a record, for example `Map<string, User>`, or when keys are not strings. `Map` accepts any value as a key and iterates in insertion order; `Set` stores unique values and also iterates in insertion order. Neither compares two separately-created object literals by structure: object identity is used, so deduplication should use an explicit key. Decide the conflict rule—first record wins, last record wins, or merge—before coding.

```ts caption="Stable first-seen deduplication for records merged from pages."
type RecordItem = { id: string; name: string };

function dedupe(items: RecordItem[]): RecordItem[] {
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  return items.filter((item) => {
    if (seen.has(item.id)) return false;
    seen.add(item.id);
    return true;
  });
}
```

Use breadth-first search: enqueue the root, repeatedly read the next node, record it if it is an element that passes the visibility rule, then enqueue its element children. A queue gives level order; a stack gives depth-first order. Avoid `shift()` in a large JavaScript queue when its removal cost is linear in the remaining array. Keep a head index instead, or use a deque abstraction, so each node is processed once and traversal is O(v + e) for a tree with v visited nodes and e parent-child edges. In real DOM code, define whether hidden subtrees, text nodes, shadow roots, and dynamically-added nodes count before implementing.

```ts caption="Breadth-first DOM traversal with an indexed queue."
function labelsByLevel(root: Element): string[] {
  const result: string[] = [];
  const queue: Element[] = [root];
  let head = 0;

  while (head < queue.length) {
    const node = queue[head++];
    if (node.matches('[role="menuitem"]')) {
      result.push(node.textContent?.trim() ?? '');
    }
    queue.push(...node.children);
  }
  return result;
}
```

Memoization caches a function's result by its inputs. For a derived list, use a key that represents every input affecting the result—such as the query, filter version, and dataset identity—and return the cached value only for the same contract. A closure over a `Map` is enough for a small utility. The trade-off is memory and invalidation: stale keys can retain large results, mutable inputs can change after caching, and an incomplete key returns incorrect UI. Prefer immutable inputs or explicit versioning, bound the cache when inputs are unbounded, and measure before adding it. Memoization reduces repeated computation; it does not make the first computation cheaper and is not a substitute for rendering only the rows that are needed.

```ts caption="Identity-keyed memoization; callers must provide stable, immutable keys."
function memoize<A, R>(fn: (arg: A) => R): (arg: A) => R {
  const cache = new Map<A, R>();
  return (arg) => {
    if (cache.has(arg)) return cache.get(arg)!;
    const value = fn(arg);
    cache.set(arg, value);
    return value;
  };
}
```

Debounce waits until calls have stopped for the delay, so it fits search requests where only the settled query matters. Throttle permits at most one invocation per interval, so it fits continuous scroll, resize, or pointer work where regular updates matter. Specify leading and trailing behavior, argument and `this` forwarding, cancellation, and whether a maximum wait is required. Clear timers on cancellation and on component teardown so the closure does not retain stale work. For visual scroll updates, keep the event handler light and batch the read/write in `requestAnimationFrame`; a timer alone does not guarantee frame-aligned rendering. The right interval is a product and performance decision, not a universal constant.

```ts caption="A trailing-edge debounce with explicit cancellation."
function debounce<T extends (...args: any[]) => void>(fn: T, wait: number) {
  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  const debounced = function (this: unknown, ...args: Parameters<T>) {
    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
  debounced.cancel = () => {
    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = undefined;
  };
  return debounced;
}
```

First define the supported value graph: arrays, plain objects, dates, maps, sets, typed data, cycles, functions, DOM nodes, and prototypes. In browsers, `structuredClone(value)` is the safer default for structured-cloneable data: it makes a deep copy, preserves circular references, and can transfer transferable objects. It throws `DataCloneError` when a value is not serializable, so it is not a universal clone for functions, many DOM nodes, or application instances with behavior. `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(value))` is a serialization round trip, not a general clone: it loses or transforms values and cannot represent cycles. If the contract is plain JSON only, serialization can be acceptable; otherwise use the platform algorithm or a deliberately scoped implementation.

```ts caption="The platform clone handles Map values and circular references."
function cloneState<T>(state: T): T {
  return structuredClone(state);
}

const state = { filters: new Map([['status', 'open']]), self: null as unknown };
state.self = state;
const copy = cloneState(state);
console.assert(copy !== state && copy.self === copy);
```

Start independent operations before awaiting them and use `Promise.all` when the screen requires every result. It preserves input order and rejects when one input rejects, but rejection does not cancel the remaining operations. Pass one `AbortSignal` to each `fetch` and call `abort()` during route change or cleanup; also treat abort as expected rather than showing an error. If partial data is useful, use `Promise.allSettled` and render each outcome. Guard state updates with the request identity or lifecycle so a slower response from an old route cannot overwrite newer data. This separates concurrency, cancellation, and stale-result protection—three concerns often accidentally conflated in frontend code.

```ts caption="Concurrent fetches with explicit response checks and cancellation."
async function loadDashboard(signal: AbortSignal) {
  const responses = await Promise.all([
    fetch('/api/profile', { signal }),
    fetch('/api/notifications', { signal }),
    fetch('/api/tasks', { signal }),
  ]);
  return Promise.all(responses.map((response) => {
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
    return response.json();
  }));
}

const controller = new AbortController();
loadDashboard(controller.signal).catch((error) => {
  if (error.name !== 'AbortError') throw error;
});
// controller.abort() on route cleanup
```

Promise reactions and `queueMicrotask` callbacks use the microtask queue. A timer callback is a task. After the current task finishes, the browser drains microtasks before returning to the event loop, so a promise callback scheduled during the task can run before a `setTimeout(..., 0)` callback. Microtasks are useful for ordering small follow-up work, but a long chain or a microtask that continually queues another microtask can delay the browser from handling input or rendering. Keep microtasks short, yield between chunks of CPU work, and use a frame-aligned or task-scheduling strategy when the work is visual or large. The key interview distinction is scheduling order versus a guarantee that the screen has already painted.

```ts caption="A small ordering example for browser task and microtask queues."
console.log('task start');

queueMicrotask(() => console.log('microtask'));
setTimeout(() => console.log('timer task'), 0);

console.log('task end');
// task start, task end, microtask, timer task
```

A DOM tree is a tree, so manual DFS is straightforward: visit a node, skip a private subtree, and recurse into children. For browser-specific traversal, `document.createTreeWalker` exposes a cursor with a `whatToShow` bitmask and an optional filter. Use `SHOW_TEXT` when text nodes are the target, and return `FILTER_REJECT` for a private element when its descendants must also be excluded. This keeps traversal policy close to the browser's node model and avoids accidentally treating text as elements. Clarify shadow-root boundaries, live mutations, whitespace-only text, and whether layout visibility is required—TreeWalker filtering by node type or attributes does not by itself answer visual visibility.

```ts caption="TreeWalker rejects private element subtrees while returning non-empty text nodes."
function publicText(root: Node): string[] {
  const walker = document.createTreeWalker(
    root,
    NodeFilter.SHOW_ELEMENT | NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT,
    {
      acceptNode(node) {
        if (node.nodeType === Node.ELEMENT_NODE) {
          return (node as Element).matches('[data-private]')
            ? NodeFilter.FILTER_REJECT
            : NodeFilter.FILTER_SKIP;
        }
        return /\S/.test(node.nodeValue ?? '')
          ? NodeFilter.FILTER_ACCEPT
          : NodeFilter.FILTER_REJECT;
      },
    },
  );
  const result: string[] = [];
  while (walker.nextNode()) result.push(walker.currentNode.nodeValue!.trim());
  return result;
}
```

Treat this as a scheduler, not `Promise.all(items.map(task))`. Maintain the next index and an active count; start at most `limit` workers, store each result at its original index, and start another item when a worker settles. Validate that the limit is a positive integer. State the failure contract: fail fast or return per-item outcomes; if fail-fast is chosen, decide whether already-started tasks are merely observed or actively cancelled. Preserve output order even though completion order differs, and accept an `AbortSignal` when the caller may navigate away. The limit protects browser and server resources; it does not guarantee a specific network throughput or make individual tasks faster.

```ts caption="A bounded-concurrency mapper that preserves input order and rejects on the first worker error."
async function mapWithLimit<T, R>(
  items: readonly T[],
  limit: number,
  task: (item: T, index: number) => Promise<R>,
): Promise<R[]> {
  if (!Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 1) throw new RangeError('limit');
  const result = new Array<R>(items.length);
  let next = 0;

  async function worker() {
    while (true) {
      const index = next++;
      if (index >= items.length) return;
      result[index] = await task(items[index], index);
    }
  }

  await Promise.all(
    Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker),
  );
  return result;
}
```
